WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.770
- Today, I want to talk about a topic

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.300
that in my humble opinion

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.310
is not talked about frequently enough

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.020
when it comes to consulting

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.580
or corporate careers in general,

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.520
and this is what it takes
to become successful

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.130
in these environments as an introvert.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.900
So welcome to another coffee break here

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.280
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.870
my name is Heinrich and on my channel

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.850
I want to help you to become successful

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.040
in the first years of your career.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.720
I'm a former McKinsey consultant

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.130
so this is where this video will be

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.420
also very much inspired from,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.660
but also from other roads
and experiences besides that.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.420
And I want to structure this
video in three main parts.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.670
First, I will talk about
what it actually means

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.720
to be an introvert

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.640
and whether I am one or not.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.520
Second, I want to talk about
some typical experiences

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.470
that you will face in consulting

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.870
based on situations that
also really happened

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:46.890
in my career.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.810
And third, I want to give you some tips,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.420
some actionable advice on what you can do

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.550
to actually survive in these environments

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.900
as an introvert.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.250
So first, let's start,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.950
what does it actually
mean to be an introvert?

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.040
And first, I think that's a
very big misunderstanding,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.110
and this is that people
think about something

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.000
like social anxiety

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.710
when they think about introverts.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.770
So introverts are people
who aren't really able

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.860
to talk in public to deliver speeches,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.730
feel super uncomfortable
with other people.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.260
Is this really what's the case?

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.510
Well, and I hope it's
not too surprising to you

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.270
that this is actually not
what at least the psychology

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.370
would say what introversion is.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.740
And the official definition
that you will find

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.530
in the textbook is that for introverts,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.100
social situations are
draining their batteries.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.550
So it takes energy to be
exposed to other people,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.020
to interact with other people,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.940
whereas extroverts, for them,

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.490
it's the opposite, so they can recharge

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.310
their batteries when they
interact with other people.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.410
And based on all of this,
I realized that yes,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.140
I am an introvert and while
I have no problem at all

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.240
with giving public speeches,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.010
I actually enjoy it to some extent,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.420
I also have no problem at all

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.670
with filming YouTube videos like these,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.280
I know that whenever I spend a full day

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.030
together with other people
interacting with them a lot,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.540
I will just feel exhausted
at the end of the day.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.850
For me, social interactions like this

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.950
surely drain my batteries

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.710
and don't recharge my batteries.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.380
So that being said, let's
move to the second part

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.640
of this video, why I think that,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.290
especially in consulting,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.230
you will face quite some
difficult situations

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.400
as an introvert.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.350
And just to give you some examples,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.350
and I just want to tell you
some anecdotes from my life,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.030
from my past experiences.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:34.560
I remember one project,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.140
I was actually the project
lead for the project.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.660
So at my team of a handful
of other consultants

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.980
who worked together with me,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.100
we were all based in the same hotel.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.310
So every single morning I was waking up

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.370
going to the hotel lobby
to pick up my breakfast.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.890
And there was actually a
project which was quite tough.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.410
So the working hours were quite long.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.260
I felt quite sleep
deprived most of the days,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.290
most of the weeks, and
especially in the morning,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.630
I still felt quite
crushed for what happened

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.160
all the days afterwards.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.870
The only thing that
actually I wanted to do

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.530
was to be for myself, pick up a breakfast,

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.830
eat that, and just try
to put myself together

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.620
and prepare myself mentally for the day.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.600
But then of course when I came down

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.720
very often I saw already
some of my other colleagues,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.300
of course, from the team sitting there

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.280
and getting some breakfast and I knew,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.510
hey, I'm the project leader,

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.180
cannot just now sit somewhere else

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.370
and let these people eat alone,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.680
but I need to join them, sit next to them.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.970
And even though this might feel

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.460
like a very small example, for me,

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.440
this was one of these examples

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.450
where you're just so also under pressure

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.940
to spend time together
with your team colleagues,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.460
with your teammates, socialize with them

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.770
even at 7:00 AM in the morning,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.020
but there's actually nothing
like you would do more

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.230
than just be for yourself, relax,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.000
and just enjoy your food.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.080
First example.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.900
Second example, team room situations.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.970
So this to some extent maybe changed

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.940
now due to the current
post corona situation,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.380
but still as a consultant,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.030
especially in the MBB consulting firm,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.760
so we spend lots of the
time at the client side

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.520
sitting in team rooms and
very often, not always,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.420
but very often, the client
gives you a rather small room,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.390
so it's not like you have
very huge meeting rooms

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.280
at your disposal all the time,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.710
to the contrary, it would not be unusual

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.690
that you sit together
with three, four, five

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.760
other people in a small room

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.880
and you barely have more space
than something like this,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.100
where you put your laptop, you are there,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.810
then often also clients come in,

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.160
maybe your partners come in

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.770
who also want a spot at the desk.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.470
It's an atmosphere where almost always

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.200
someone will be constantly on the phone

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.050
doing conference calls and video calls

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.140
in parallel where you
are there trying to work.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.120
It will be very noisy.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.470
This environment alone
for a full blown introvert

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.480
is just pure stress.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.420
It is very difficult just to go somewhere,

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.010
just have time for yourself

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.170
often then in these client environments,

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.640
you're looking for some rooms,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.070
some empty rooms where you maybe have

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.750
a little bit of space to potentially do

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.760
a very important confidential call,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.930
but not always there will be

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.480
while I'm sure you kind of
just go there for hours,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.620
just to work, right?

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.620
So second situation why
this might be very stressful

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.300
for an introvert.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.000
Third situation, social interactions

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.400
beyond just the work during the day.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.800
There are many examples for that.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.660
A rather typical one would be that

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:34.740
once or twice a week,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.900
usually someone senior from a leadership,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.480
someone like a partner, is flying in

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.630
and spends the day or
half of the day with you.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:44.910
And it would not at all
be unlikely that then

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.310
in the evening you would together go out

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.460
for a team dinner to
spend the time together.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.710
And really to some extent,
this is very enjoyable.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:54.300
Often you go to nice restaurants,

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.950
you have very nice food,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.360
and probably more often than not,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.270
this can also be a lot
of fun, of course, again,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.270
potentially also depending
on what your personality is.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.730
But here the truth is also that, again,

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.910
very often the days
before, the weeks before,

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.410
you had very little sleep,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.880
you feel quite exhausted, you
feel quite sleep deprived.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.380
And probably there are other things

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.630
that you would likely to like to do more

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.820
than I'll spend the
evening or several hours

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.000
at least together with potentially
your senior leadership,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.920
your colleagues, especially if you know

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.630
that once you get back,

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.150
once it's 10:00 PM, 11:00 PM or even 8:00

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.030
and you get back, there are
still work to do, right?

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:35.070
And I can tell you this would not at all

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.900
be an unlikely situation.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.570
And I'll try to put
yourself in the situation,

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.010
there's the partner who of course

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.390
is often a very social person,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.550
wants to interact with you, talk to you.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.010
Often they're in different project,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.810
one project, different associates

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.000
sitting at the desk, sitting at the table,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.460
and especially when I
was a junior associate,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.680
I often feel more or less pressed

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.300
that I now need to interact,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.460
that I now need to say something,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.520
talk to the partner, somehow
entertain the person,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.370
and maybe to some extent
that wasn't really needed,

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.840
but then on the other hand, of course,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.780
there's also this topic of visibility

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:11.940
and how important that is to get promoted,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.230
to get to the next level.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.540
I will link somewhere above here a video

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.000
where I discussed this concept in detail.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.650
But again, in a nutshell,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.690
think about try to put
yourself into the shoes

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.460
of a partner, maybe every half a year,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.530
there is then the evaluation season

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.390
and that partner needs to decide

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.670
what associate gets what ranking,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:30.870
what performance ranking,

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.460
which determines your bonus,

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.250
and which also determines
the speed of your promotions.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.210
Well, who is more likely
to get their promotion

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.580
if the people or if the
different associates

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:42.930
got very similar evaluations,

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.340
for instance, from the project lead?

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.520
Is it the person whom this
partner vividly remembers

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.490
from regular team interactions,
team dinners like this?

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.250
Or is the person that in
all these interactions

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.830
just sat there, didn't contribute much,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.860
didn't do anything to
just somehow be remembered

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.670
really by the partner
in a significant way?

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.880
Well, I hope it's not surprising to anyone

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.430
that very likely it's the former person

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.450
who has the advantage,

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.070
which of course also creates

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.980
exactly the pressure to
socially interact like this

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.100
and then further depleting
your batteries more and more

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.920
throughout the course of
such an already stressful day

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.900
in consulting.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.120
Next example, office activities.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.120
So the typical rhythm
while working in consulting

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:25.740
is that from Monday to Thursday,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.830
you're really fully invested
in your client work,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.180
needs to do lots of stuff off,

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:31.710
and you're also located client site,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:32.940
not always, but often.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.150
But then on Friday often it's
a bit of a more slower day,

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.310
at least traditionally you
would have had the opportunity

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:40.350
to move back to your home location,

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.120
spend the day in the office,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.790
or actually just to start to spend the day

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.530
in the home office.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.320
So now every Friday
you had the opportunity

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.840
to either go home, spend the day at home,

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.130
potentially do a little bit of work,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.450
maybe some office stuff,

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.490
or you go to the office.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.010
Well, why might it be a good
idea to go to the office?

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.010
Well, of course, because
you meet other colleagues,

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:03.990
you have the opportunity to network,

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.160
often also,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.020
different office societies,

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.050
office events are taking
place on the Friday,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.150
so this is the opportunity again,

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.520
to expose yourself to social situations,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.110
to network in your office,

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.550
to get to more and more people,

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:19.950
potentially also network
with the senior leaders

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.380
who often also are there on the Fridays.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.690
And again, now you guessed it, right?

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.120
So for whom will it be likely easier

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.850
to decide to not just
spend the day at home

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:32.460
and relax from already
the very stressful time

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:33.870
you had from Monday to Thursday,

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:35.730
the introvert or the extrovert?

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.250
I trust it's clear for whom
this is the case, right?

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:39.510
But again, on the other hand side,

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.970
missing all the Fridays activities always

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.520
surely is not helping you in your career,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.540
again, we just discussed
the topic of visibility.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.580
And last but not least,
very much related to that

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.700
is of course also
relationship with clients,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.130
especially if you are based client side,

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.680
sitting at the same
location with your clients.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.060
Of course, during the day,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.920
you will have all types of meetings,

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.560
some more formal interactions
with the clients.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.060
And surely this is your goal,

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.860
it is your duty as a consultant,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.660
to some extent you're also evaluated

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:11.130
whether you're able to do that or not

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.730
to build relationships at
least with the most important

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.340
clients relative to
know the project topic,

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:18.900
the things that you're working on.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.700
And of course, in order to do that,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.130
the best people usually
are people who are able

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.860
beyond just the very
standard formal meetings

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.160
that they have to potentially
just walk by their office,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.200
pick up a cup of coffee with them,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.210
maybe just invite them for lunch,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.880
spend time with them, have
some more talk with them

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.760
and build exactly these
types of relationships.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.610
And all of this together leads to

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.740
I think the fact that
often for introverts,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.390
especially in the first years,

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.760
it might be a bit more
challenging to get used

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.750
to this consulting environment
to thrive and to succeed

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.490
and to get the best evaluations

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.680
to be fast track promoted

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:57.420
and so on and so forth.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.100
So all this being said,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.900
while I acknowledge all the struggles

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.030
and I personally very much failed

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:04.920
all these struggles as well,

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:07.680
I do truly think that you
can succeed in consulting

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.580
independent whether you are
an introvert or an extrovert.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.950
So let me now share a couple of tips,

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.040
some strategies that I
also picked up over time

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.970
in order to succeed in these
roads as an introvert indeed.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.700
And first, I want to
talk about just learning

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.360
some of these more typical
extroverted behaviors

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.640
because yes, as an introvert,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.200
you can develop yourself,

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.530
you can learn to also adopt
some of these behavior patterns.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.140
They might feel less natural to you,

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.440
it might feel a bit more
challenging and harder to you,

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:41.580
but it is possible.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.340
And there are many things now
that I can can talk about.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.200
I just want to point to one thing,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.380
and this is the so-called trust equation.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.390
And I have created a
video about this as well,

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.190
I will link it somewhere above here

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:54.300
where you can check it out.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.970
It is a simple framework
of what you can do

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:00.750
to build trust with
colleagues, with clients.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.390
It discusses several
actions that you can pursue.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:04.890
And all of them, or most of them,

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.560
don't really even depend
on spending lots of

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.990
social time with them, chit
chatting a lot with them,

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:12.570
so I think these opportunities
do really also succeed

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.430
and build trust with these people,

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:18.480
even with a more
introverted type of mindset.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.450
Second, it's not the case
that you always just need

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.760
to do small talk with everyone

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:26.550
because typically introverts
don't really enjoy doing that.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.533
Typically, introverts like to have

00:12:28.533 --> 00:12:30.990
even more meaningful conversations.

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:34.200
And here I think it's a
helpful mindset to adopt

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.140
that you can actually
derive off in a conversation

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.080
exactly in the direction
that you would like, right?

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.210
And if you don't want to do small talk,

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.270
don't do small talk and
rather move to the topics

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.427
that are more meaningful to you.

00:12:47.427 --> 00:12:49.800
And in my humble experience,

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.630
what I've seen often also the other person

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.190
actually enjoys this to have this

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.500
a bit more unusual conversation with you

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.100
beyond just always talking
about how the last weekend was

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.710
or maybe what your plans
for the next weekend are.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.510
And so what might you do?

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.570
There's one saying that
that sticks to my mind

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.400
that I find quite inspiring

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.570
or let me know in the comments

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.910
whether you agree with this or not,

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:14.880
but this saying says that simple minds

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.690
talk about other people, mostly negative,

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:21.690
mediocre minds talk about experiences,

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:26.690
mostly positive, and great
minds talk about ideas.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.550
So instead of chit-chatting
about other colleagues,

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.280
instead of just talking
about your last weekend,

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.080
what the experience was like,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.850
just try to talk about ideas.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.260
So maybe something that you read

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:40.740
that really inspired you,
that sparked some ideas

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:42.900
in your mind and what the
opinion of the other person is.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.610
I think this is always a great share,

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.500
talking about books that you're reading,

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.360
other things like this.

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.880
If you do want to talk about experiences

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:53.520
well, maybe don't make it
about the last weekend,

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.890
but, for instance, a
question that I always

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.470
like that I find always
listening quite interesting

00:13:58.470 --> 00:13:59.850
responses, something like,

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.970
so what was your most memorable experience

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.490
while working at this
employer for the last year?

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:08.040
What was your most memorable experience

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.120
doing this or that?

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.950
I think that's a great
question also to ask

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:12.780
maybe to a more senior
colleague, to a partner,

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:16.290
what was your most
memorable experience here

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:17.910
working in this consulting firm?

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:21.540
Always fascinating what
types of responses you get.

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:23.760
Or again, instead of
talking about the weekend,

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:25.740
maybe ask something like,

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.420
what are you looking forward to

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.040
in the next weeks or months?

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.663
What are you excited about?

00:14:31.632 --> 00:14:33.240
And this is great
because then these people

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.580
also start thinking about
maybe positive emotions,

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.810
positive feelings,

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:39.030
thoughts that I might have going forward.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:41.190
Great, nice little question that I feel

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:45.450
can also help you to go beyond
the standard small talk.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:47.760
Next problem that often
the introverts are facing

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.400
is that often as an introvert you just set

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:54.630
a very high bar for
yourself in order to talk.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:56.700
So you feel like in order for you

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.160
to have the permission to talk,

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.660
the thing that you want to say

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.940
needs to be insanely valuable,

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:05.400
and if what you want to
say is not very valuable,

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.110
then you don't really
have the right to talk,

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.090
and then you're often just confused,

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.410
and maybe even annoyed.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:11.970
But all these other people around you

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:13.560
then often were extroverted people

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.060
who just say things where you felt

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:17.550
like that was absolutely unneeded,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.260
not even worth your time,

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.640
but many people do that.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.560
And in order to overcome this,

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.870
of course, the obvious
thing is to lower the bar

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.060
a little bit in the
sense that not you saying

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.590
useless stuff all the time,

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:31.290
but that often you are
very much underestimating

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.510
the actual value of the things

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:34.350
that you want to contribute.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.090
So maybe you feel like
a thought that you have

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:37.860
is obvious to everyone else,

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:39.390
which is why you don't need to share it,

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.850
but very often the truth
is that this thought

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.920
that you are having is not at all obvious

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.900
to at least some people in the room,

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.340
which is why many more things

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.800
very likely are worth to be shared

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.300
than you might think.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.910
And one little technique
that I find quite helpful,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.810
you hear what some people
call the barrier of silence.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.510
And this is that often during
the course of a meeting,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.850
the longer the meeting continues

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.430
without you having said anything,

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.350
the harder it will be for you

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:11.250
to now suddenly, after the
meeting is already one hour in,

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:15.030
to only now start talking
and start saying something

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:16.320
for the very first time.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.980
So this barrier of silence
is building up over time

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.690
the longer the meeting gets,

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:23.430
which is why a typical recommendation

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.650
is that you should try
in the very beginning,

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.050
just at the start of the
meeting to say something,

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.110
interact with someone, just
drop a couple of comments,

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:33.870
couple of remarks, even
if maybe they're not

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.790
the most valuable ones ever.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.040
But if you do this, then this barrier,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.920
this perceived hurdle
for you to talk again,

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.350
engage again, will feel much lower,

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.030
much more easy to overcome

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.070
than if you wouldn't have said anything

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:50.460
for the very first significant
part of the meeting.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:53.310
Next tip to succeed as an introvert

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:56.430
is to focus more on your strengths.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:58.860
And of course this now
taps into some cliches,

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.720
and of course introverts
are also different,

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:03.390
and not every introvert
behaves in the same way,

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:04.860
but at least some of the strengths

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.860
that are typically
associated with introverts

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.740
is active listening,
that maybe you don't have

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:12.600
the need to talk all the time yourself,

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:15.090
but actually you're a really good listener

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:18.180
and you can detect perceived things

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.590
while just listening to someone

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:22.140
that maybe someone else who is louder

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.120
or who he or she might be louder

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.100
might not be able to do this.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.630
So active listening, build on that,

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:30.630
obviously this is a
very, very crucial skill

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.130
in professional services.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:33.693
Second, empathy.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:36.330
Here again what many
people seem to believe

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:38.700
is that introverts are more empathetic

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.500
because they have these antennas

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:43.440
for the feelings, for the
emotions of other people,

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:44.850
which are easy maybe to be overheard

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.220
if you just focus too much
and just talking yourself,

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.620
use that empathy is so important,

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:52.080
so crucial for professional services,

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.810
it is a people's business, it
is a relationship business.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.850
And last but not least,
again, maybe a cliche,

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:58.290
but many people seem to believe

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:02.070
that introverts are also
more analytically inclined,

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:03.390
more analytically able.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.490
Again, of course this
is a very valuable skill

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.750
in consulting as well.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:09.270
So yes, maybe you won't
perform to the level

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.310
as other people do in
some of the other areas

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:12.990
where the extroverts are stronger.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.450
But then just double
down on your strengths,

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:18.720
try to really excel in the
path where you are good at.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:20.340
And of course you can then also get all

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:22.170
the positive evaluations,

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:23.640
all the fast tech promotions

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.557
and all these things you might fancy also

00:18:25.557 --> 00:18:29.970
based on such virtues,
strengths that you can pay.

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:31.830
All right, and of course,
last but not least,

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.650
what's really important
then to you is the weekends.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:36.900
You need to recharge your
batteries at some point,

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:39.600
very likely for Monday to
Thursday then Monday to Friday,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.910
You won't really have
the ability to do that.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.660
At some point, you should just
accept that if this is you,

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.020
if this is your personality,

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:49.470
probably Saturday and Sunday,
it'll be very challenging

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:52.140
for you to now also then just
do lots of social things,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:54.300
spend lots of time with your friends,

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:55.470
go out in the evenings.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:56.630
I mean, if this is great for you,

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.890
if this feels like the
right thing to do for you,

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:00.630
of course feel free to do that.

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.580
But for introverts, I
think for many of us,

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:05.370
for me again included,
the weekend is was often

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:07.230
the time where I just slept in,

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:09.900
where I just enjoyed a slower day

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.590
and rather not engaging too
much with many other people.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.570
And at some point you might need to accept

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.040
that this is just what you need to do

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.930
in order to properly prepare yourself

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:23.130
for then the next rather
busy and challenging week.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.770
So this is my thoughts on introverts,

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:27.060
please let me know in the comments

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:28.590
is this your experience as well?

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:30.990
I would always be very interested also

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:32.940
in some suggestions that you might have

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:34.350
on how to deal with this.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:35.580
My name is Heinrich.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:37.050
Thanks to all the
members of Firm Learning,

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.050
please leave a like if you
took any value out of this.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:42.180
Subscribe if you like this type of content

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:43.380
and want to see more.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.284
Looking forward to the
next video next weekend.

00:19:45.284 --> 00:19:46.143
Bye bye.

